    <div class="hbadge rv in">Journal · Guide</div>
    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">What Changes at Sea in Madeira Between Winter and Summer?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>3 December 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Late summer water is about 5 °C warmer than late winter water, most big Atlantic swells arrive between October and March, and Funchal&#x27;s sunset shifts from about 18:00 in December to about 21:15 in June. The animals, the light and the crowds change with them.</p>

      <h2>What changes between winter and summer, at a glance?</h2><p>Here is the short version, for the south coast around Funchal.</p><table><thead><tr><th>At sea</th><th>Winter, December to March</th><th>Summer, June to September</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sea temperature</td><td>about 18 to 20 °C</td><td>about 21 to 24 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Sunset in Funchal</td><td>about 18:00 to 19:30</td><td>about 20:00 to 21:20</td></tr><tr><td>Sunset Trip leaves</td><td>about 16:45 in December</td><td>about 20:00 in June</td></tr><tr><td>Swell</td><td>Atlantic storms send big north-west and west swells, mostly to the north coast</td><td>Smaller swells, steady north-east trade winds, south coast usually sheltered</td></tr><tr><td>Midday UV index</td><td>about 3 to 6</td><td>about 8 to 10</td></tr><tr><td>Port and crowds</td><td>Cruise ship season, with a crowded bay at New Year</td><td>Fewer cruise ships, more families on holiday</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>How warm is the sea in winter and in summer?</h2><p>Cool but swimmable in winter, warm in late summer. Water temperature bottoms out at about 18 °C between February and April and peaks at about 23 to 24 °C between August and October. It lags a month or two behind the air, so the water in September is warmer than in June.</p><p>For most people 18 °C means a quick, bracing swim, and 23 °C means staying in as long as you like. On the <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a>, a winter swim at Fajã dos Padres is usually a short one, and the paddle boards come into their own. Our guide to <a href="/posts/madeira-sea-temperature-swimming-guide">Madeira's sea temperature</a> goes through it month by month.</p><h2>When are the waves biggest?</h2><p>From autumn to early spring. That is when Atlantic depressions pass to the north and send long north-west and west swells towards Madeira. The north and west coasts take the biggest seas. In one December 2024 warning, the maritime authority expected swells of 2.5 metres on the north coast but no more than 2 metres on the south. The island itself shelters the south coast from the north, but south-west swells, and swell bending round the west end, can still reach it.</p><p>In summer the pattern changes. North-east trade winds blow steadily from about May to September, and the mountains put the south coast in their lee. Swells are smaller, and the coast west of Funchal is often at its calmest. The wind is strongest at the island's exposed corners, such as the east end near the airport.</p><p>Winter does not mean no trips, but it does mean more days when the skipper says no. When the skipper does call off a Chifbay trip, you pick a new date or take a full refund. If you are weighing up a winter booking, read <a href="/posts/are-boat-trips-in-madeira-worth-it-in-winter">is a boat trip in Madeira worth it in winter</a>.</p><h2>Which animals turn up in which season?</h2><p>Some are here all year, and some come and go. According to local whale-watching guides, the usual pattern is this:</p><ul><li><strong>Bottlenose dolphins</strong>: all year, more often from March to October.</li><li><strong>Short-beaked common dolphins</strong>: mostly from December to May, the winter and spring dolphin.</li><li><strong>Atlantic spotted dolphins</strong>: mostly from March to November, the summer dolphin.</li><li><strong>Bryde's whales</strong>: mostly from April to October, often in coastal waters.</li></ul><p>None of this is a promise, and Chifbay's trips are not wildlife tours. Dolphins can turn up in any month, and when they do, the same 50 metre rule applies all year. For the full picture, see our guide to <a href="/posts/dolphins-and-whales-in-madeira-guide">dolphins and whales in Madeira</a>.</p><h2>How do the light and the sunset change?</h2><p>By more than three hours. Sunset in Funchal comes at about 18:00 in early December and about 21:15 in late June. Daylight runs from about 10 hours at the winter solstice to more than 14 hours at the summer one. The clocks go back an hour at the end of October and forward at the end of March, which shifts everything by an hour overnight.</p><p>That is why the <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a> has no fixed time. Departure is set 75 minutes before sunset, so in December the boat goes at about 16:45 and in June at about 20:00. The direction changes too. Seen from the sea off Funchal, the December sun sets in the west-south-west, out over open water. The June sun sets in the west-north-west, behind the line of the coast. In winter the low sun also stays warm and golden for longer through the afternoon.</p><h2>Is the sea busier in winter or in summer?</h2><p>The port is busier in winter, the beaches in summer. Funchal's cruise season runs roughly from autumn to spring, and the port handled 331 cruise calls in 2025. New Year is the big night: eleven cruise ships came into the bay for the fireworks at the end of 2025. On 31 December 2026 Chifbay has its own <a href="/new-years-eve-fireworks-boat-madeira">New Year's Eve fireworks night on the boat</a>, out on the bay from 22:00 to 01:00.</p><p>In summer there are fewer cruise ships, but July and August bring school holidays from across Europe, and the beaches are at their busiest. On the water that matters less than on land, because a boat can simply move on if a cove is crowded.</p><h2>So which season is better on the water?</h2><p>It depends on what you want from the day. Summer is better for long swims, paddle boarding, late sunsets and the calmest south coast. It is worse if strong sun bothers you, because the midday UV index reaches about 10. Winter is better for soft light, early sunsets that leave the evening free, cooler air and the chance of common dolphins. It is worse if you get seasick easily or want warm water, because there are more lumpy days and the sea is about 18 °C.</p><p>One honest warning for winter: if your holiday has only one possible day for a boat trip, the odds are against you more often than in summer. Keep a second date free in case the skipper calls it off.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can you swim in the sea in Madeira in winter?</h3><p>Yes. The sea stays at about 18 to 20 °C from December to March, which is cool but fine for a short swim for most people.</p><h3>When is the sea calmest in Madeira?</h3><p>Usually in summer, from about June to September, when the north-east trade winds leave the south coast in the island's lee and swells are smaller.</p><h3>What time is sunset in Funchal in winter?</h3><p>About 18:00 in early December, moving to about 19:15 by mid-March.</p><h3>Are there more dolphins in winter or in summer?</h3><p>Different ones. Common dolphins are seen mostly from December to May, Atlantic spotted dolphins mostly from March to November, and bottlenose dolphins all year.</p>

      <blockquote>Winter gives you the light, summer gives you the water. The south coast gives you both, just not on the same day.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

